A Streamlit-based tracker for U.S. Congress stock transactions. It monitors publicly disclosed House and Senate trades, tracks selected politicians and their transactions, and provides alerts when new trades are reported.

Data pipeline
-------------
fetch_trades.py runs daily (see .github/workflows/fetch-trades.yml) and pulls
from a free, keyless, no-rate-limit feed (kadoa-org/congress-trading-monitor's
public/data/trades.json, sourced from official House Clerk / Senate eFD /
OGE filings). It writes the merged dataset to data/trades.json, which
index.html / app.js reads directly. Search always runs over the full
dataset; the table just paginates the matches 50 at a time so it stays
fast to render. The "Top 5 Whale Trades" dashboard highlights the largest
disclosed trades (by dollar amount) from the last 30 days.

Email alerts
------------
Visitors can subscribe themselves from the "Email Alerts" tab on the site:
pick one or more politicians, enter an email, and "Continue on GitHub"
opens a pre-filled GitHub issue (titled "Subscribe request: <email>").
process_subscription.py (run by .github/workflows/process-subscriptions.yml
on every new issue with that title) validates the request - the email
must look real and every politician must match a filer name already in
data/trades.json - appends valid entries to alerts.json, commits it,
comments on the issue with the result, and closes it. Invalid requests get
a rejection comment explaining why and are left open for the submitter to
fix. A submitter needs a free GitHub account to open the issue; anyone can
technically open one against the repo, so treat alerts.json as low-trust
data (a bogus entry is just a JSON line to revert).

alerts.json can also be edited by hand, same format:

   [
     { "member": "Nancy Pelosi", "email": "you@example.com" }
   ]

   "member" must match the filer name exactly as it appears in
   data/trades.json (case-insensitive).

To actually send the mail, add these repository secrets (Settings ->
Secrets and variables -> Actions) so fetch-trades.yml's alert step can
reach an SMTP server:

   - SMTP_HOST     e.g. smtp.gmail.com
   - SMTP_PORT     e.g. 587 (optional, defaults to 587)
   - SMTP_USERNAME your SMTP account / email address
   - SMTP_PASSWORD an app password (Gmail: Google Account -> Security ->
                    2-Step Verification -> App passwords). Don't use your
                    normal account password.
   - SMTP_FROM     optional, defaults to SMTP_USERNAME

Without those secrets set, send_alerts.py simply skips sending mail (the
daily workflow still runs and updates trades.json normally).
